WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.140
The website says, Free Mint. But the request
underneath can give someone

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:08.500
permission to move every NFT in a
collection. I'm Mohamed Adib, a

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.940
student at Sri Venkateswara College of
Engineering. This is PlainSign: a

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.680
way to understand that permission before
approving it.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.720
Here is the permission in this test example.
PlainSign pauses the request

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.080
and shows the address that would get access.
It explains why that

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.240
permission is dangerous. If I choose Reject,
it returns the wallet's

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:33.640
standard rejection error. The request never
reaches the wallet. The

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:37.940
Continue button forwards the original
request unchanged. The separate demo

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.680
shows that interception working.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.000
A signature with no gas fee still deserves
attention. This Permit2

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.240
example grants an unlimited token permission
for a long time. A normal

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:53.500
sign-in message has a different meaning.
PlainSign decodes both and shows

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.560
the difference. Green means no major warning
signs were found under the

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.180
rules; it is not a guarantee of safety.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:05.400
The decision comes from open rules. We
decode the transaction or

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.260
signature, add public chain information, and
explain the rules that fired.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:14.260
This public version does not use AI to write
the explanation. It has no

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:18.740
backend account or telemetry. These are
inspectable heuristics, and provider

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.520
interception is best effort.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:25.160
The September prototype came before this
competition, and that history is

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:29.420
disclosed. The October work adds public
contract data from the main Ethereum

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.900
network. It keeps warnings about known
malicious addresses visible during an

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.220
RPC outage. It also gives judges a
walkthrough they can try without a

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:42.580
wallet. The walkthrough uses simulated
metadata. Mainnet validation

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:45.660
uses public reads, not real asset
transactions.

00:01:46.877 --> 00:01:50.877
For individuals, the extension and rules
stay free and open source.

00:01:51.557 --> 00:01:55.037
The proposed business is a software package
that wallets and apps can add to

00:01:55.037 --> 00:01:59.237
their products. It would include maintained
rules, policy controls, and

00:01:59.237 --> 00:02:03.637
support. I still need to test demand and
pricing. I am not claiming

00:02:03.637 --> 00:02:05.177
customers or revenue today.

00:02:06.257 --> 00:02:10.677
The next test is simple: watch five people
use the demo. Can they

00:02:10.677 --> 00:02:14.757
explain the permission before deciding?
Where do warnings confuse them?

00:02:15.057 --> 00:02:19.357
How long does the decision take? Then I want
to find two wallet or

00:02:19.357 --> 00:02:23.437
app teams for a small integration pilot.
Those are planned experiments,

00:02:23.437 --> 00:02:24.697
not current traction.

00:02:25.817 --> 00:02:29.437
The goal is to help people make a clear
decision at the moment it matters.

00:02:30.117 --> 00:02:33.457
You can try the sample requests without
installing a wallet, and inspect the

00:02:33.457 --> 00:02:37.317
code and rules in the repository. My next
milestones are independent

00:02:37.317 --> 00:02:41.657
security review and observed user tests.
PlainSign helps people

00:02:41.657 --> 00:02:43.677
understand the permission, then decide.
